      "content": "Mitosis is the process by which a cell divides into two daughter cells that are genetically identical to the parent cell. It's a crucial part of growth, development, and reproduction in living organisms. Imagine you have a pair of identical twins - each twin gets a copy of the same DNA, and that's basically what happens during mitosis.\n\nThe process of mitosis can be broken down into several stages, each with its own unique role in ensuring that the new cells receive the correct amount of genetic material. Let's take a closer look at each stage:\n\n**Prophase**: During this stage, the chromatin (the complex of DNA and proteins that make up chromosomes) condenses into visible chromosomes. This helps you understand how the genetic material is organized and ready for division. The nuclear envelope, which separates the chromosomes from the rest of the cell, begins to break down. The centrioles, which are small structures near the nucleus, start to move apart and form a spindle apparatus. This apparatus is made up of microtubules that will eventually help move the chromosomes to opposite poles of the cell. By the end of prophase, you'll have a clear picture of how the cell is preparing for division.\n\n**Metaphase**: In",
